11. Limitations to address in reporting
As the article discusses (Al Mohajer et al. 2021):

state measures introduced during the intervention period reduced community transmission and may confound the result, although hospital infections fell faster than community cases;
adherence to other infection-prevention measures was not assessed;
without individual-level data, effects by role or by unit cannot be examined;
testing uptake rose during the surge, which may confound the positivity series;
face shield tolerability was not measured.

Why face shields
On 6 July 2020 the hospital required every healthcare worker to wear a face shield on entering the facility, for encounters with patients and with other staff; goggles were allowed for those who could not tolerate a shield. The article gives the reasons (Al Mohajer et al. 2021):

a face shield protects the eyes, nose and mouth from droplets;
it may stop the wearer touching their face, reducing self-inoculation;
it may act as a barrier between staff, and from staff to patients;
it is cheap (about US$2 when 3D printed locally), reusable and easy to clean.

The article also cites a simulation study in which a face shield greatly reduced exposure to a simulated cough at close range.
